{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_015zudb5H3HJBWeqF8bSCFqu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0161CZUC7wKgQnDYYt4vz6od","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513/projects/agent_progress_probe/docs/paper/sections/related_work.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7300,"cache_read_input_tokens":9038,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7300,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_014gVQX82GDH4gjf6y1yHnKj","session_id":"d074e59e-e78e-4d2c-a1a9-c5b03b4aa2ca","uuid":"efaa0242-ad63-4ae1-99e2-3ac383e0093b"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0161CZUC7wKgQnDYYt4vz6od","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Related Work}\n2\t\\label{sec:related_work}\n3\t\n4\t\\paragraph{Process Reward Models.}\n5\tPRMs assign step-level quality scores to guide agent behavior \\citep{xi2025agentprm,choudhury2025prm,fan2026agentrm}, with benchmarks revealing degradation on long-horizon trajectories \\citep{wang2026planrewardbench} and the advantage of specialized over general-purpose PRMs \\citep{li2026toolprmbench}.\n6\t\\citet{le2022coderl} take a complementary value-function approach operating on program states.\n7\tOur work differs in operating at window level without outcome labels, targeting real-time monitoring ($<$5\\,ms) rather than training signal generation.\n8\t\n9\t\\paragraph{Agent Self-Reflection.}\n10\tSelf-reflection methods \\citep{shinn2023reflexion,yao2023tot,zhou2024lats} prompt agents to diagnose their own failures, but LLMs cannot reliably self-correct without external feedback \\citep{huang2024selfcorrect,kamoi2024selfcorrection}.\n11\tWe address the prior question---\\emph{when} is the agent stuck---using external structural signals.\n12\t\n13\t\\paragraph{Weak Supervision for Sequential Data.}\n14\tData programming \\citep{ratner2016dataprogramming,ratner2017snorkel} enables dataset creation through labeling functions, extended to sequential data via linked HMMs \\citep{safranchik2020linkedhmm}, multi-resolution LFs \\citep{sala2019multiresolution}, and heterogeneous aggregation \\citep{lison2021skweak}.\n15\tWe apply this paradigm to agent trajectories, finding---unlike \\citeauthor{safranchik2020linkedhmm}'s NER results---that temporal label models do not improve over majority vote in our low-conflict 3-LF regime (\\S\\ref{sec:temporal_label_models}).\n16\t\n17\t\\paragraph{Noisy Labels and Label Leakage.}\n18\tOur silver labels connect to noisy label learning \\citep{han2018coteaching,li2020dividemix}, though our noise is temporally block-correlated rather than instance-level.\n19\tThe structural feature--label overlap instantiates label leakage \\citep{kaufman2012leakage} and Goodhart's Law \\citep{manheim2018goodhart}; we mitigate this through cross-coding-agent evaluation and a human-annotated gold set (\\S\\ref{sec:gold_set}).\n20\t\n21\t\\paragraph{Agent Monitoring and Degradation.}\n22\t\\citet{orlanski2026slopcodebench} document coding agent degradation; \\citet{chen2025monitoring} establish that sequence-level context is necessary for monitoring; \\citet{kale2025weaktostrong} show weak monitors can oversee stronger agents; and \\citet{ma2024agentboard} introduce fine-grained progress tracking.\n23\t\\citet{cemri2025multiagentfail} and \\citet{zhu2025agentfail} catalog systematic failure modes including cascading failures in long-horizon tasks.\n24\tWe complement these with a systematic comparison demonstrating that lightweight structural features suffice for detection and transfer.\n25\t\n26\t\\paragraph{Cycle Detection and Process Mining.}\n27\tCycle detectors \\citep{george2025cycledetection,pipis2025reasoningloop} target structural repetition but miss semantically diverse stagnation; \\citet{liu2026trajad} train trajectory verifiers for broader anomaly detection.\n28\tOur structural features draw on process mining \\citep{vanderaalst2016processmining}, and the success of hand-crafted features in cross-coding-agent transfer parallels classical findings where feature-based methods outperform learned representations for narrow detection tasks \\citep{liu2008isolationforest}.\n29\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_014gVQX82GDH4gjf6y1yHnKj","session_id":"d074e59e-e78e-4d2c-a1a9-c5b03b4aa2ca","uuid":"7ebf1611-953c-42dc-a865-38884f83d997","timestamp":"2026-05-15T15:30:56.813Z"}
